// The package holds two projects: the renderer (`tsconfig.json`, React and
// bundler resolution) and the Electron side (`tsconfig.electron.json`, NodeNext).
// An editor looks up from the file it opens for the nearest `tsconfig.json` that
// covers it, finds the renderer one — which includes `src` and `shared` only —
// and puts everything under `electron/` in a default project instead: node10
// resolution, which cannot read the `exports` map of a workspace package.
// `@hw/headless-blender-mcp` then reads as a missing module in the editor while
// `tsc` is perfectly happy.
//
// This file is that nearest config. It adds nothing of its own: the settings,
// the includes and the output paths all stay in `tsconfig.electron.json`, which
// is what the build and `yarn typecheck` still use. Relative paths in an
// extended config resolve against the file that declared them, so both configs
// describe exactly the same project.
{
  "extends": "../tsconfig.electron.json"
}
